  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Useful as a carrier but not as the failure model itself: the envelope preserves exact bytes, dispatch IDs, and mandatory signatures, so Bob can keep verifiable local evidence and higher layers can wrap or log a refusal. But it does not define ring membership, next-hop authorization, refusal records, or reconfiguration behavior, so the core bakeoff question is mostly deferred to payload or wrapper protocols.",
    "strengths": [
      "Clear dispatch boundary: unknown payloads stay opaque and speculative parsing is forbidden.",
      "Canonical DAG-CBOR bytes plus mandatory signature slots support durable local evidence.",
      "pCID-based layering supports sparse knowledge and avoids a central registry."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No native notion of ring membership or authorized next hop.",
      "Does not standardize whether authorization failure breaks the ring, skips a hop, records refusal, or reconfigures membership.",
      "Multi-hop authorization evidence likely needs wrappers or payload-specific chaining beyond the base envelope."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Opaque forwarding of unknown payloads can conflict with per-hop authorization unless policy forbids relay without handler-level checks.",
      "Different payload protocols may encode refusal or reconfiguration differently, hurting cross-simulation comparability and audit.",
      "The specimen is still draft and unfrozen, so long-term wire semantics are not yet settled."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Which payload or wrapper protocol defines Bob's authorization check and signed refusal record?",
      "What policy prevents opaque relay when Bob cannot evaluate per-hop authorization for an unknown pCID?",
      "How are skip-hop or ring-reconfiguration decisions represented and audited without a central authority?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
